  "notes": "Standard Mandarin (Pǔtōnghuà), Beijing-based. THIS SPEC READS PINYIN, NOT HANZI — its graphemes are Pinyin units and the input contract is Pinyin. Nothing here reads a Han character, and nothing could: a Han character does not encode sound, so no rule engine can pronounce one. That limit is stated by `zh-Hani`. Pinyin, being an alphabet, is fully transcribable — which is why the romanization is the tractable orthography and the native script is not. Going from Hanzi to this spec needs a dictionary (CC-CEDICT), not rules.\n\nBoth ways of writing tone are read: the diacritics of the official orthography (⟨mā má mǎ mà⟩) and the ASCII tone digits ⟨1 2 3 4⟩, with ⟨0⟩ and ⟨5⟩ for the neutral tone. The diacritic forms are listed as whole rimes rather than as marked letters, because the mark sits inside the rime — on ⟨a⟩ where the rime has one, otherwise on ⟨o⟩ or ⟨e⟩, otherwise on the last vowel letter — so reading a marked letter on its own would break the rime it belongs to. The Chao values are Chao's (1968): 55, 35, 214, 51, and nothing for the neutral tone. ⟨v⟩ is the usual ASCII stand-in for ⟨ü⟩.\n\nFour Pinyin spelling conventions hide a segment and are stated here rather than left to a letter-by-letter reading. Pinyin drops the umlaut of ⟨ü⟩ after ⟨j q x y⟩, where /u/ and /y/ do not contrast, so ⟨u un uan ue⟩ read /y yn ɥɛn ɥe/ in that environment and /u wən wan/ elsewhere. ⟨y⟩ and ⟨w⟩ are not consonants but the zero-onset spellings of the medials, so ⟨yi wu yu⟩ are the bare vowels /i u y/ and ⟨ye yan yin ying yun yue yuan⟩ spell the same rimes as ⟨ie ian in ing ün üe üan⟩. ⟨o⟩ after ⟨b p m f⟩ spells the rime /uo/ with the medial unwritten, while ⟨o⟩ standing alone is the interjection [ɔ]. And ⟨i⟩ after ⟨z c s⟩ and after ⟨zh ch sh r⟩ is not /i/ at all but the apical vowel — dental [ɹ̩] in the first series, retroflex [ɻ̩] in the second — which this spec writes /ɨ/ and gives both apical realizations as its allophones (Duanmu 2007; Lin 2007). Prenuclear high vowels are read as the glides /j w ɥ/ rather than as vowel medials (Duanmu 2007).\n\nTwo kinds of syllable that no other Pinyin spelling reaches are listed on their own. ⟨ê⟩ is the final ㄝ /ɛ/ written alone — the third note to the Scheme's table of finals says to spell it that way — so the letter turns up only in the interjection 欸, the same final being written plain ⟨e⟩ inside ⟨ie üe⟩. And the interjections 嗯 ⟨ng⟩, 哼 ⟨hng⟩ and 噷 ⟨hm⟩ have no vowel letter at all; they read /ŋ xŋ xm/, with ⟨hng⟩ and ⟨hm⟩ carrying no tone and ⟨ng⟩ and ⟨m⟩ taking one on the nasal letter itself (Lee 2023, from the seventh edition of the 现代汉语词典). Without these entries a letter-by-letter reading drops ⟨ê⟩ and reads ⟨ng⟩ as /nk/.\n\nThe retroflex suffix ⟨-r⟩ of erhua is a stated gap: it is read as a plain /ɻ/ coda, and the rime reshaping it triggers is not modelled. The syllable ⟨er⟩ itself is /ɚ/.\n\nAgainst the ipa_childes Mandarin gold, four transcription conventions account for most of the remaining distance and are not defects on either side. That gold is produced by the pinyin_to_ipa table, so the row measures agreement with another Pinyin→IPA convention rather than accuracy. It writes the retroflex series ⟨t̠ʃ̺ ʃ̺⟩ where this spec writes ⟨ʈʂ ʂ⟩; it gives the third tone as 313 where Chao's value is 214; it places the tone letter after the nucleus and before a nasal coda, where IPA writes it at the syllable edge; and it writes ⟨aŋ⟩ where this spec writes the backed [ɑŋ].",
